---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ExcelScreenshot from "../../components/ExcelScreenshot.astro";
import SpreadsheetLab from "../../components/guides/SpreadsheetLab";
import SpreadsheetConcepts from "../../components/guides/SpreadsheetConcepts";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title="Your first spreadsheet cipher solver">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 01 / BUILD SOMETHING USEFUL</p>
    <h1>
      Move the letters.
      <br />
      Make a message<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Never used a spreadsheet? Start here. We’ll learn one small idea at a time, then use it to
      build a cipher-solving grid.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-meta">
      <span>EXCEL OR GOOGLE SHEETS</span>
      <span>PAUSE AT ANY CHECKPOINT</span>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <a href="#puzzle">Start with a puzzle</a>
      <a href="#cells">Warm-up: cells and formulas</a>
      <a href="#mid">Choose a letter with MID</a>
      <a href="#grid">Lesson 1: a five-column grid</a>
      <a href="#move">Move the columns</a>
      <a href="#flexible">Lesson 2: different widths</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <h2 id="puzzle">What are we trying to solve?</h2>
      <p>
        Suppose a friend writes <strong>LET’S MOVE LETTERS</strong>. Remove the spaces and
        apostrophe and you have 15 letters: <code>LETSMOVELETTERS</code>. Put five letters in each
        row:
      </p>
      <pre> L E T S M
 O V E L E
 T T E R S</pre>
      <p>
        Your friend swaps the columns around. The first row becomes <code>S E M T L</code>; the
        others become <code>L V E E O</code> and <code>R T S E T</code>. Read those rows across and
        the secret message is <code>SEMTL LVEEO RTSET</code>. No letter has changed into a different
        letter: only its position changed. That is a <strong>transposition</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        To undo it, we need to try different column orders. Moving a whole column changes all three
        rows at once, which is much quicker than rewriting every letter. Have a go below before
        opening a spreadsheet.
      </p>
      <SpreadsheetLab client:load />
      <p>
        Our project will build that movable grid in Excel or Google Sheets. We’ll begin with five
        columns, then later make the number of columns adjustable. This particular puzzle repeats
        one rearrangement in every block of five letters, so it is called a{" "}
        <strong>block transposition</strong>. Other transpositions read down columns instead; this
        guide tackles the row-by-row puzzle we have just tried.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cells">Warm-up: find your way around</h2>
      <p>
        A spreadsheet is a page of small boxes called <strong>cells</strong>. The letters across the
        top name the columns. The numbers down the side name the rows. Cell <strong>B3</strong> is
        where column B and row 3 meet.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          In Excel, choose <strong>Blank workbook</strong>. In Google Sheets, choose{" "}
          <strong>Blank spreadsheet</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Find the tab at the bottom, usually called Sheet1. Double-click its name, type{" "}
          <strong>Basics</strong> and press Enter. A tab is a separate page inside your spreadsheet
          file.
        </li>
        <li>
          Click cell B3: column B, row 3. You should see B3 in the small <strong>name box</strong>{" "}
          above the grid, on the left. You can also click that box, type B3 and press Enter to jump
          there.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="empty"
        alt="Excel worksheet with column letters, row numbers, the name box and sheet tabs"
        caption="Find the name box at the top left and the Basics tab at the bottom. Our later lesson tabs are also visible; you only need Basics for this warm-up."
      />
      <p class="checkpoint">
        <strong>Check:</strong> you can point to a cell, a row number, a column letter, the name box
        and the sheet tab. If you click the wrong cell, simply click the right one.
      </p>

      <h3>1. Ask the spreadsheet to calculate something</h3>
      <p>
        A <strong>formula</strong> is an instruction that starts with <code>=</code>. The
        spreadsheet works it out and shows the answer in the cell.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Click B3. Type <code>=1+2</code> and press Enter. B3 should show <strong>3</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Click B3 again. The long <strong>formula bar</strong> beside <em>fx</em>, above the grid,
          shows <code>=1+2</code>. The cell shows the answer; the formula bar shows how you got it.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="first-formula"
        alt="B3 shows 3 while the formula bar shows equals 1 plus 2"
        caption="B3 contains an instruction, not a typed answer. Compare the 3 in the cell with =1+2 in the formula bar."
      />

      <h3>2. Let one cell use another cell’s value</h3>
      <p>
        Later we want one message cell to feed every letter in our grid. A{" "}
        <strong>cell reference</strong> lets a formula look up a value somewhere else.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Type <code>10</code> in A3.
        </li>
        <li>
          Replace B3 with <code>=A3+1</code>. Press Enter. The answer is <strong>11</strong>: “take
          the value in A3 and add 1”.
        </li>
        <li>
          Try changing A3 to 20. B3 changes to 21 without retyping its formula. Put A3 back to 10
          before the next step.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="reference"
        alt="A3 contains 10 and B3 contains 11 from the formula A3 plus 1"
        caption="The formula in B3 points to A3. Changing A3 changes the answer in B3."
      />

      <h3>3. Copy an instruction instead of retyping it</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Put <code>20</code> in A4. Click B3, copy with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>, click B4, and
          paste with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>. On a Mac, use Command instead of Ctrl.
        </li>
        <li>
          Click B4 and look at the formula bar. It now says <code>=A4+1</code>, so the answer is 21.
          Because you copied the formula one row down, its reference moved one row down too.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="copying"
        alt="B4 shows 21 and its copied formula uses A4 instead of A3"
        caption="Copying can adjust references for you. B3 uses A3; B4 uses A4."
      />
      <p>
        Sometimes we want that movement. Sometimes we don’t. Dollar signs{" "}
        <strong>pin a reference in place</strong>: <code>$A$3</code> means “keep column A and row 3
        when I copy this formula”.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Type <code>=$A$3+1</code> into B6.
        </li>
        <li>
          Copy B6 to C6. Both cells should show 11. Click C6: it still uses <code>$A$3</code>. We’ll
          use this trick to keep every letter formula pointing at the same message.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="fixed-reference"
        alt="B6 and C6 both show 11 because the copied formula keeps the fixed A3 reference"
        caption="The dollar signs keep the source cell fixed when the formula is copied across."
      />
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        <strong>Pause here if this is new.</strong> You have learned cells, formulas, references and
        copying. Explain the difference between A3 and $A$3 to a partner before building the cipher
        grid.
      </aside>

      <h2 id="mid">How do we take just one letter?</h2>
      <p>
        Our grid needs one letter per cell, but our ciphertext is one long string. We need an
        instruction that can fetch “the first letter”, “the second letter”, and so on. That
        instruction is the <strong>MID function</strong>. A function is a named instruction with
        some inputs inside brackets.
      </p>
      <pre>=MID("SEMTL",2,1)</pre>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>The three inputs to MID, in order</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Input</th>
              <th>What it tells MID</th>
              <th>In this example</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>Text</th>
              <td>Where to take characters from</td>
              <td>
                <code>"SEMTL"</code> — quotes mark text you are typing directly
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Start</th>
              <td>Which character to begin at, counting from 1</td>
              <td>
                <code>2</code> — the E
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>How many</th>
              <td>How many characters to take</td>
              <td>
                <code>1</code> — just E
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <SpreadsheetConcepts part="mid" client:load />
      <ol>
        <li>
          Return to Basics. Click A9 and type <code>=MID("SEMTL",2,1)</code>. Press Enter. The
          answer should be E.
        </li>
        <li>
          Try changing 2 to 5. Then try start 2 and count 3. Predict the answer before pressing
          Enter.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="mid"
        alt="MID in A9 takes the second character from SEMTL and returns E"
        caption="MID selects the character you ask for. The formula bar shows all three inputs."
      />

      <h2 id="grid">Lesson 1: build a five-column grid</h2>
      <h3>4. Give the ciphertext its own page</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Click the <strong>+</strong> beside the sheet tabs at the bottom. Double-click the new
          tab’s name, type <strong>Input</strong>, and press Enter.
        </li>
        <li>
          In Input, put <code>Ciphertext</code> in A1 and <code>SEMTL LVEEO RTSET</code> in A2.
        </li>
        <li>
          Put <code>=SUBSTITUTE(A2," ","")</code> in A3. It means “take A2 and replace each ordinary
          space with nothing”. A3 should show <code>SEMTLLVEEORTSET</code>. We remove the display
          spaces so our character positions count only letters.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="input"
        alt="Input tab shows ciphertext in A2 and its space-free version in A3"
        caption="A3 is the message our grid will use. It contains 15 letters and no spaces. Double-click the right edge of column A’s heading if its text needs more room."
      />
      <h3>5. Fetch the first letter</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Click + again and name the new tab <strong>Grid</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Type 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 into A1, B1, C1, D1, E1. These labels will help us recognise the
          original columns later.
        </li>
        <li>
          In A2 type <code>=MID(Input!$A$3,1,1)</code>. The answer is S. <code>Input!</code> means
          “on the Input tab”; <code>$A$3</code> pins the message cell; the final two 1s mean “start
          at character 1 and take one character”.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="grid-first"
        alt="Grid has labels 1 to 5 and A2 fetches the first character S from the Input tab"
        caption="One successful letter is enough for this checkpoint. The next step will ask the spreadsheet to choose positions for us."
      />

      <h3>6. Let the column number choose the letter</h3>
      <p>
        We could type five nearly identical MID formulas, changing the start number each time.
        Instead, <code>COLUMN()</code> tells us which column a cell is in: A gives 1, B gives 2, and
        so on. It has empty brackets because it needs no extra input here.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Replace A2 with <code>=MID(Input!$A$3,COLUMN(),1)</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Copy A2. Select B2:E2 by dragging from B2 to E2, or type <code>B2:E2</code> in the name
          box and press Enter. Paste. A colon means “the rectangle from this cell to that cell”.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="grid-row"
        alt="The first grid row shows S E M T L and E2 uses COLUMN to choose character 5"
        caption="Your first row should read S E M T L. Each copied formula still reads the same message, but COLUMN() supplies a different start position."
      />

      <h3>7. Work out positions for the next rows</h3>
      <p>
        The next row must start at character 6, not character 1 again. Before row 3 there is one
        complete row of five letters. Before row 4 there are two. So a character’s position is:
      </p>
      <pre>position = column number + (complete rows before this one × 5)</pre>
      <p>
        Our first row of letters is worksheet row 2. <code>ROW()</code> gives the worksheet row, so{" "}
        <code>ROW()-2</code> gives 0, 1, 2 for our three letter rows. Multiplication is written{" "}
        <code>*</code> in a spreadsheet.
      </p>
      <SpreadsheetConcepts part="index" client:load />
      <ol>
        <li>
          For a moment, replace A2 with <code>=COLUMN()+(ROW()-2)*5</code>. Copy A2, select A2:E4
          using the name box, then paste.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check the numbers: row 2 should show 1–5, row 3 should show 6–10, and row 4 should show
          11–15. These are the character positions we need.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="positions"
        alt="Grid displays character indices 1 to 15, with B3 calculating position 7"
        caption="B3 is column 2 in the second row of letters: 2 + (3 − 2) × 5 = 7. Make sure your numbers match before adding MID."
      />
      <h3>8. Give that position to MID</h3>
      <p>
        Now use our position calculation as MID’s middle input. We are joining two things you
        already know: calculating a position, then fetching the character at that position.
      </p>
      <pre>=MID(Input!$A$3,COLUMN()+(ROW()-2)*5,1)</pre>
      <ol>
        <li>Put this formula in A2.</li>
        <li>
          Copy A2, select A2:E4 and paste. Your grid should now contain the three scrambled rows,
          with one letter in every cell.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="grid-complete"
        alt="The complete grid contains SEMTL, LVEEO and RTSET in three rows"
        caption="Check all three rows: SEMTL / LVEEO / RTSET. The formula bar for B3 shows how character 7 was selected."
      />

      <h2 id="move">Try column orders by moving the columns</h2>
      <h3>9. Make a movable copy</h3>
      <p>
        Our formulas use the cells’ row and column positions. If we drag those formulas into
        different columns, they may calculate new letters. We want the letters to travel with the
        columns instead. A <strong>values-only copy</strong> keeps the answers and leaves the
        formulas behind.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Add a tab named <strong>Solve</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          On Grid, select <code>A1:E4</code> and copy. Go to Solve and click A1.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Excel:</strong> choose Home → the arrow under Paste → Values (the icon with 123).{" "}
          <strong>Google Sheets:</strong> choose Edit → Paste special → Values only. The shortcut in
          Sheets is Ctrl+Shift+V.
        </li>
        <li>
          Click Solve!A2. The formula bar should show just <code>S</code>, without an equals sign or
          MID. You now have a movable copy.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="movable"
        alt="Solve contains a values-only copy, and the formula bar shows S rather than MID"
        caption="This is the important check before moving anything: the formula bar contains a letter, not a formula."
      />
      <h3>10. Move a whole column</h3>
      <p>
        Click the <strong>E column heading</strong> above the grid, not the number 5 inside E1. The
        whole column becomes selected. Its first cell has our original label 5.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="select-column"
        alt="The whole E column is selected on Solve before being moved"
        caption="Select the letter E at the very top. You will move its label and all three letters together."
      />
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Excel:</strong> point at the border of the selected column until the pointer
          becomes a four-way move arrow. Hold <kbd>Shift</kbd>, drag the border to the left edge of
          column A, and release the mouse before releasing Shift. Shift inserts the column between
          others.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google Sheets:</strong> click the column heading, then drag that heading to the
          left of A. Release when the vertical guide line is in the right place.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If dragging is awkward, use the keyboard/menu alternative in Excel: cut the selected column
        with Ctrl+X, right-click the destination column heading, then choose{" "}
        <strong>Insert Cut Cells</strong>. In Sheets, right-click a column heading and use{" "}
        <strong>Move column left</strong> repeatedly. These insert the column rather than
        overwriting another one.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="first-move"
        alt="The original fifth column is now first, and the column labels read 5 1 2 3 4"
        caption="After moving the original column 5 to the front, the labels should read 5, 1, 2, 3, 4. Those numbers let you track the original columns even though the worksheet headings change."
      />
      <h3>11. Keep experimenting, then read the message</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Try more moves. Read each row from left to right, continuing onto the next row. Does it
          begin to look like words?
        </li>
        <li>
          For our example, move the column labelled 2 into second place, then the column labelled 4
          into third place. The labels should be <strong>5, 2, 4, 1, 3</strong>.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="solved"
        alt="Solve now has labels 5 2 4 1 3 and rows LETSM, OVELE and TTERS"
        caption="Read LETSM, then OVELE, then TTERS: LET’S MOVE LETTERS. The same column order has helped every row."
      />
      <h3>12. Join the answer into one cell</h3>
      <p>
        You can read the grid already. <strong>TEXTJOIN</strong> is useful when you want a single
        answer to copy. Its inputs are the separator, whether to ignore empty cells, and the cells
        to join.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          In F2 enter <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A2:E2)</code>. Empty quotes mean no separator; TRUE
          tells it to ignore empty cells.
        </li>
        <li>Copy F2 down to F4. Each F cell joins one row.</li>
        <li>
          In F6 enter <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,F2:F4)</code>. It joins the three row answers, giving{" "}
          <code>LETSMOVELETTERS</code>.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="joined"
        alt="Column F joins each row, and F6 contains LETSMOVELETTERS"
        caption="Joining one row at a time makes the reading order clear in both Excel and Sheets."
      />
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        <strong>You have built a working solver.</strong> Stop here and try another five-column
        puzzle. Keep Grid’s formulas and make a fresh values-only copy on Solve for each attempt.
      </aside>

      <h2 id="flexible">Lesson 2: what if the puzzle has a different width?</h2>
      <p>
        The first solver always puts five letters in each row. For a four-column puzzle we would
        need to change the formula, copy a different rectangle and make another movable copy. That
        is fine occasionally, but awkward if we want to try several widths.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let’s put the width in one cell instead. Changing that cell will redraw the grid. In this
        reusable version we’ll give the grid its own row and column numbers. The formulas will
        follow those numbers when we move a column, so we can experiment directly in the live grid.
        We will no longer need a values-only copy of the letters on Solve.
      </p>
      <h3>13. Give the reusable sheet its inputs</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Return to <strong>Input</strong>. Put <code>Width</code> in A5 and <code>5</code> in B5.
          Keep our original message in A2 and the space-removing formula in A3. The letters in A3
          should still be <code>SEMTLLVEEORTSET</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Add a new tab named <strong>Sequences</strong>. Keeping the message and width on Input
          means they stay in one place while we move columns on this new tab.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="sequence-inputs"
        alt="Input has the original message in A2, cleaned letters in A3 and width 5 in B5"
        caption="The two inputs live on Input: change A2 for a new message and B5 for a new width."
      />
      <h3>14. Generate our own row and column numbers</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>SEQUENCE</strong> makes a list of numbers for us. Its inputs are
        <code>SEQUENCE(how many rows, how many columns, first number, step)</code>. If we leave out
        the first number and step, both are 1. The answers spread into nearby empty cells from the
        cell containing the formula. This is called <strong>spilling</strong>. Both modern Excel and
        Google Sheets can do this; we do not need Excel’s special spill-reference symbol.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Two directions for our helper numbers</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Cell on Sequences</th>
              <th>Formula</th>
              <th>What it makes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>C5</th>
              <td>
                <code>=SEQUENCE(1,20)</code>
              </td>
              <td>One row: 1, 2, 3, …, 20 across C5:V5.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>A6</th>
              <td>
                <code>=SEQUENCE(100,1,0)</code>
              </td>
              <td>One column: 0, 1, 2, …, 99 down A6:A105.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li>
          On Sequences, type <code>Rows before</code> in A5. Enter the two formulas from the table.
          Leave B and W empty: our movable columns will be C to V, with a blank column on each side.
        </li>
        <li>
          Look down A. The first row of letters has <strong>0 complete rows before it</strong>, the
          next has 1, then 2. Starting at zero saves us having to subtract the worksheet row number.
        </li>
        <li>
          Make only the column labels movable: select C5:V5 and copy. In Excel use
          <strong>Paste Special → Values</strong> over the same cells. In Sheets use
          <strong>Edit → Paste special → Values only</strong>. Press Escape when finished. Keep A6’s
          vertical SEQUENCE formula live.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        A horizontal SEQUENCE is one connected block: we cannot move just one of its generated
        cells. This one-time values-only paste turns the 20 labels into independent numbers. We will
        keep the letter formulas live and move them with their labels. There is no need to copy the
        letter grid.
      </aside>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="sequence-axes"
        alt="Sequences has column labels starting at C5 and row counts starting at zero in A6"
        caption="Check the two directions: 1, 2, 3… across row 5; 0, 1, 2… down column A. B stays empty."
      />
      <p>
        SEQUENCE needs Excel for Microsoft 365, Excel 2021 or newer, or Google Sheets. If an older
        Excel version shows a name error, type 1 and 2 in C5:D5, select both and drag the fill
        handle across to V5. Likewise fill 0 and 1 from A6:A7 down to A105. The remaining formulas
        work the same way.
      </p>
      <h3>15. Build the position from those numbers</h3>
      <p>
        We still need a position for MID. In the second row of a width-5 grid, source column 3
        contains character 8: skip 1 complete row × 5 letters, then take letter 3. Our helper cells
        give us every part of that calculation.
      </p>
      <pre>=C$5+$A6*Input!$B$5</pre>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Enter this in C6. <code>C$5</code> is the source-column label above us; <code>$A6</code>
          is the number of complete rows before us; <code>Input!$B$5</code> is the width. The answer
          is 1 + 0 × 5 = <strong>1</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Copy C6 across to G6, then copy C6:G6 down to row 8. Check for 1–5, then 6–10, then 11–15.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The dollar signs pin only what must stay fixed when copying: row 5 for a label, column A for
        a row count, and both parts of Input’s width cell. There is no ROW or COLUMN in this
        formula.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="sequence-positions"
        alt="The helper-number formula produces positions 1 to 15 in three rows"
        caption="The helper numbers reproduce the same character positions as Lesson 1, without depending on a letter column’s worksheet position."
      />
      <h3>16. Fetch letters and prepare widths up to 20</h3>
      <p>
        Replace C6 with <code>=MID(Input!$A$3,C$5+$A6*Input!$B$5,1)</code>. The new part wraps our
        position calculation in MID, using the cleaned message on Input. Copy across to G6 and down
        to row 8: SEMTL / LVEEO / RTSET should return.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also want room for wider puzzles. <strong>IF</strong> chooses between two answers:
        <code>IF(test,answer if true,answer if false)</code>. A source-column label larger than the
        width should give empty text, rather than another letter.
      </p>
      <pre>=IF(C$5&gt;Input!$B$5,"",MID(Input!$A$3,C$5+$A6*Input!$B$5,1))</pre>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Replace C6 with this formula. Copy it, select C6:V105 using the name box, and paste. This
          prepares 20 columns and 100 rows. At width 5, labels 6–20 give blank cells.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="sequence-grid"
        alt="The live grid starts in C6, with row counts in A and inactive columns blank"
        caption="SEMTL / LVEEO / RTSET is back. The blank cells to the right still contain formulas, ready for a larger width."
      />
      <h3>17. Join between two fixed edges</h3>
      <p>
        Leave B and W blank. Joining from B to W includes all 20 letter columns between them. Those
        two outside edges stay in place when we move columns inside the grid, so the answer
        continues to include every letter.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          In X6 enter <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,B6:W6)</code>. Copy it down to X105.
        </li>
        <li>
          In X2 enter <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,X6:X105)</code>. This joins the rows in order.
        </li>
        <li>Use the name box to jump back to C6. Now we can try some column moves.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>18. Move the formula columns themselves</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Move the whole worksheet column whose row-5 label is <strong>5</strong> before the first
          letter column, C. Use Excel’s Shift-drag at the column edge, or drag the column heading in
          Sheets, just as in Lesson 1. Keep the label and all its formulas together.
        </li>
        <li>
          Move label 2 into second place and label 4 into third place. Keep every moved column
          inside C:V. Leave A, the empty edge columns B and W, and the answer column X in place.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="sequence-solved"
        alt="The formula columns have been moved to labels 5 2 4 1 3 and show LETSM OVELE TTERS"
        caption="The letters follow their source-column labels. We moved live formulas, with no values-only copy of the letters."
      />
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="sequence-answer"
        alt="X2 contains LETSMOVELETTERS after moving the live formula columns"
        caption="Jump to X2 using the name box. The joined answer has updated after the moves."
      />
      <h3>19. Check a different width</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          On Input change B5 to <code>3</code> and A2 to <code>ABCDEFGH</code>. A3 updates for us.
          On Sequences rearrange the columns so labels 3, 1, 2 come first, followed by the others.
          You can also type a new complete order in row 5, using each number 1–20 once.
        </li>
        <li>
          X2 should show <code>CABFDEGH</code>. The last row has no third source letter, so its
          first displayed cell is empty; G and H still join correctly. Labels larger than 3 stay
          blank wherever they have been moved.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        series="lesson"
        name="sequence-three"
        alt="The same formulas at width 3 give CAB, FDE and a blank followed by G and H"
        caption="The formulas handle another width and a short last row. Check CAB / FDE / GH, then try your own column order."
      />
      <p>
        There is one limit to remember: we prepared 100 rows, so at width 3 the sheet holds 300
        letters. In X3 enter{" "}
        <code>=IF(LEN(Input!A3)&gt;100*Input!B5,"Add more rows","All letters fit")</code>. LEN
        counts characters. If the message is too long, extend the grid, the row joins and the final
        joining range together, and increase the number of rows in A6’s SEQUENCE.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary>Troubleshooting</summary>
        <p>
          Some spreadsheet settings use semicolons instead of commas between function inputs. If a
          formula appears as text, remove any leading apostrophe, choose General/Automatic number
          format and re-enter it. If a copied formula fetches the wrong message, check its dollar
          signs. If letters repeat or disappear after reordering, check that each active
          source-column number appears exactly once. Your joined answer should contain as many
          letters as the input.
        </p>
      </details>
